  "answer_text": "**The Mom Test** is a popular product design and customer development methodology created by entrepreneur Rob Fitzpatrick, detailed in his 2013 book, *\u201cThe Mom Test: How to talk to customers & learn if your business is a good idea when everyone is lying to you.\u201d*\n\nAt its core, it is a framework for conducting customer interviews to get honest, unbiased feedback, helping entrepreneurs, product managers, and researchers validate their ideas before investing time and money into building them.\n\n---\n\n### Why is it called \"The Mom Test\"?\nThe name comes from a simple thought experiment: if you pitch your new business idea to your mom, she will almost certainly tell you it\u2019s a wonderful, world-changing idea. She doesn't do this to deceive you; she does it because she loves you and wants to protect your feelings. \n\nMost potential customers will act exactly like your mom\u2014they will politely nod, tell you your idea \"sounds cool,\" and give you false encouragement to avoid being rude or hurting your feelings. \n\n**The Mom Test is a way of asking questions so that even your mom couldn't lie to you.**\n\n---\n\n### The 3 Golden Rules of The Mom Test\nThe entire framework relies on three fundamental principles:\n\n#### 1. Talk about their life, not your idea\nDo not pitch your product, and try not to mention your idea at all. If you say, *\"I'm building an app that does X, what do you think?\"* the conversation immediately shifts to validation and politeness. Instead, ask them about their current routine, their life, and the specific problems they encounter. \n\n#### 2. Ask about specifics in the past, not generics about the future\nPeople are terrible at predicting their future behavior, but past behavior rarely lies. \n* **Bad:** *\"Would you use an app that does X?\"* (They will say yes, but never actually download it).\n* **Good:** *\"How did you handle this problem the last time it happened? What did you do?\"* (This reveals whether the problem is actually painful enough to warrant a solution).\n\n#### 3. Talk less and listen more\nYour goal is to learn, not to sell. The more you talk about your product, the more you bias the user's answers. Keep your questions short, and let the customer fill the silence.\n\n---\n\n### Good vs. Bad Questions: Examples\n\n| Avoid These (Bad Questions) \u274c | Ask These Instead (Good Questions)  | Why? |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| *\"Do you think this is a good idea?\"* | *\"How do you currently solve this problem?\"* | Opinions are useless; only facts about current behavior matter. |\n| *\"Would you buy a product that does X?\"* | *\"What else have you tried to solve this?\"* | If they haven't actively tried to solve the problem in the past, they won't pay for your solution. |\n| *\"How much would you pay for this?\"* | *\"How much does this problem cost you right now in time or money?\"* | Hypothetical budgets are made up on the spot. Real, ongoing costs show actual pain points. |\n| *\"Would you like a feature that does Y?\"* | *\"Tell me about the last time you ran into that issue.\"* | Customers will always say \"yes\" to more features, leading to bloated, useless products. |\n\n---\n\n### Red Flags to Watch Out For\nWhen talking to potential customers, Fitzpatrick warns to watch out for \"bad data\" that can mislead you:\n\n* **Compliments:** If someone says, *\"That's so smart!\"* or *\"I love that idea!\"* your brain registers success. In reality, compliments are often just a polite way of ending a conversation. **Ignore compliments** and steer back to concrete facts.\n* **Feature Requests / \"If Only\" statements:** Customers will say, *\"I'd use it if only it had a calendar integration.\"* This is a trap. It shifts the burden of their inaction onto your product. Instead, ask: *\"How do you manage your calendar right now?\"*\n* **Hypotheticals:** Any answer containing *\"I would,\" \"I will,\"* or *\"I might\"* is a guess, not data.\n\n### The Ultimate Validation: Commitment\nThe final step of the Mom Test is pushing for **commitment**. Polite praise is cheap, but actual commitment proves true interest. At the end of a conversation, look for:\n* **Financial commitment:** A deposit, pre-order, or signing a letter of intent.\n* **Time commitment:** Booking a follow-up meeting, agreeing to a trial, or spending 2 hours setting up a test.\n* **Reputational commitment:** Agreeing to introduce you to their boss, legal team, or purchasing department.",
